Exactly. Audience effect is another overlay that cuts across nearly every performance stack.

The same underlying capability can express very differently depending on who is watching, what the performer thinks the audience expects, and how much consequence they attach to judgment. In psychology, parts of this show up as social facilitation, evaluation apprehension, choking under pressure, and self-presentation.

You can map it as:

baseline ability → perceived audience → arousal → attentional shift → behavioral modulation → observed performance

And the modulation can go either direction. A crowd can increase energy, commitment, pain tolerance, speed, expressiveness, and risk-taking. But it can also increase muscle tension, self-monitoring, hesitation, overthinking, conformity, and error.

So I’d add a broader layer above the stacks:

Capability × State × Environment × Audience × Stakes = Expressed Performance

That distinction matters because observed performance is not identical to underlying capacity.

Someone can be:

* excellent privately and mediocre under observation,
* mediocre privately and unusually good when there’s a crowd,
* technically capable but distorted by evaluation,
* or so practiced that the audience becomes fuel rather than interference.

And it recurses. The athlete has spectators. The speaker has listeners. The comedian has laughter. The leader has followers. The trader has other traders. Even conversation changes when a third person enters the room.

The especially interesting variable is the imagined audience. Nobody even has to be physically present. A person can perform for a coach, parent, rival, future reader, camera, God, social media, or an internalized critic who exists entirely in their model of other minds.

So audience belongs beside things like fatigue, pressure, novelty, incentives, uncertainty, and identity as a context operator acting on the stack rather than another isolated skill.